  <!-- Main Slide Stage -->
  <main class="stage">

<section class="slide active" data-title="1. Title"><div><div class="kicker emerald">NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026 · Team Mana Mana</div><h1 class="editable">Find Earth analogs for Moon &amp; Mars research</h1><p class="subtitle editable">A tool for finding terrestrial field sites and explaining which properties make them useful for a mission experiment.</p><div class="grid-3"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">20,540</h3><p class="editable">Unique Earth locations across lab samples and terrain survey windows</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">149</h3><p class="editable">Mars measurement records</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">12</h3><p class="editable">Moon measurement records</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="2. Challenge"><div><div class="kicker emerald">The research problem</div><h2 class="editable">Different experiments need different field sites</h2><p class="subtitle editable">A researcher testing a mapping rover may need similar terrain and lighting. A researcher validating a spectrometer may need similar sample chemistry.</p><div class="grid-3"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Terrain</h3><p class="editable">How steep, varied or connected is the surrounding landscape?</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Chemistry</h3><p class="editable">How closely do the measured oxide proportions compare?</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Lighting</h3><p class="editable">Can a modeled Earth daylight geometry approximate a selected planetary scenario?</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="3. Explorer"><div><div class="kicker emerald">The prototype</div><h2 class="editable">Select a measurement and adjust the comparison</h2><p class="subtitle editable">Choose a Moon or Mars measurement. Start with Terrain only, then switch to Mixed, Geochemistry-oriented or Terrain-oriented, or edit the weights directly.</p><div class="grid-2"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Find candidates</h3><p class="editable">Filter by Earth region or site pool. Turn on Best match per country or territory to see the leading candidate from each displayed region.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Inspect the evidence</h3><p class="editable">Click a result to see the map location, source links and individual feature distances under Why this match? When available, <strong>Explain with Gemini</strong> summarizes the selected comparison in plain language alongside the scores. The explorer computes the ranking before Gemini is called.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="4. Method"><div><div class="kicker emerald">Reproducible ranking</div><h2 class="editable">From measurements to an explainable ranking</h2><p class="subtitle editable">The explorer recalculates distances for the selected target and comparison settings. A smaller distance means a closer match under those settings.</p><p class="subtitle editable"><strong>Why these features?</strong> Terrain helps screen areas for robotic landing and rover mobility. Sunlight geometry helps select lighting conditions for camera-based mapping. Chemistry and mapped geology help select materials and field sites for geochemical research.</p><div class="grid-3"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Chemistry + terrain</h3><p class="editable">Compare chemistry with centered log ratios. Scale chemical and terrain differences by Earth interquartile ranges and summarize each group with a bounded RMS distance.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Geology + sunlight</h3><p class="editable">Compare mapped units using a documented rubric where supported. Optional sunlight compares modeled horizontal solar projections.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Weights + coverage</h3><p class="editable">Combine group distances using normalized weights. Unavailable target groups are omitted and remaining weights renormalized. Chemical ranking requires the selected measured components.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="5. Data"><div><div class="kicker emerald">Source datasets</div><h2 class="editable">Measurements, elevation and maps</h2><p class="subtitle editable">The repository documents source products, coverage and reproducible builds for the explorer.</p><div class="grid-3"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Earth</h3><p class="editable">Lab datasets include GSJ, USGS, Geological Survey Ireland and Geoscience Australia. Terrain uses NASADEM and Copernicus DEM GLO-30. Regional rock types come from GLiM.<br><br><strong>Canada · CSA RADARSAT-1:</strong> Historical radar imagery links provide context for selected Earth sites. The ranking uses elevation-derived roughness.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Mars + Moon</h3><p class="editable">Planetary measurements are paired with MOLA or SLDEM2015 terrain and USGS geological maps. JPL SPICE supports modeled solar geometry.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Documentation</h3><p class="editable"><a style="color:var(--accent-cyan)" href="https://github.com/romimana/Planetary-analogs/blob/main/DATA_CODEBOOK.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data codebook ↗</a><br><a style="color:var(--accent-cyan)" href="https://github.com/romimana/Planetary-analogs/blob/main/TERRAIN_SURVEY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terrain survey ↗</a><br><a style="color:var(--accent-cyan)" href="https://github.com/romimana/Planetary-analogs/blob/main/SOURCES.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and terms ↗</a></p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="6. Features"><div><div class="kicker emerald">What the explorer compares</div><h2 class="editable">Four groups answer four different questions</h2><p class="subtitle editable">The terrain metrics summarize 5 × 5 km windows using nominal 500 m bins. Geochemical measurements represent sampled material. Mapped geology provides regional context.</p><div class="grid-2"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Terrain</h3><p class="editable">Median and 90th-percentile slope, share steeper than 10°, elevation relief, local roughness and largest connected gentle area.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Measured chemistry</h3><p class="editable">SiO₂, TiO₂, MgO, CaO, K₂O and MnO. Compare the measured set selected for the experiment.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Mapped geology</h3><p class="editable">Compare regional map units using the rubric where calibrated. Historical RADARSAT-1 links provide imagery context.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Sunlight</h3><p class="editable">Compare geometric projections onto a horizontal surface and show paired reference times in UTC.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="7. Live demo" id="live-demo"><div><div class="kicker emerald">Live demonstration</div><h2 class="editable">Planetary Analogs Explorer</h2><p class="subtitle editable">Choose a measurement, rank with Terrain only, then keep the target fixed and rerank with Mixed or Geochemistry-oriented. Open a result to compare its feature distances and source evidence. Then, if the button is available, click <strong>Explain with Gemini</strong> to show how the selected result is explained in plain language.</p><p><a class="action-btn primary" href="https://planetary-analogs.onrender.com/#explorer">Open live explorer ↗</a></p><p class="subtitle">After the demo, use the browser Back button and advance to Limitations.</p></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="8. Limitations"><div><div class="kicker emerald">Interpretation</div><h2 class="editable">What a high-ranking site can support</h2><p class="subtitle editable">The ranking supports early site screening. Field validation depends on the experiment and the scale at which the equipment interacts with terrain.</p><div class="grid-3"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Terrain resolution</h3><p class="editable">The regional metrics cannot resolve individual boulders, wheel-scale roughness or touchdown hazards.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Coverage and comparability</h3><p class="editable">Ranking depends on the measured targets, valid Earth candidates and active groups. Sample materials and laboratory methods vary by source.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Environmental fidelity</h3><p class="editable">Sunlight uses modeled historical reference times. Local shadows, atmospheric attenuation and actual illumination forecasts require additional data.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="9. Next steps"><div><div class="kicker emerald">Future work</div><h2 class="editable">From candidate sites to field tests</h2><p class="subtitle editable">Validate top candidates against the requirements of a specific experiment.</p><div class="grid-2"><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Verify the field site</h3><p class="editable">Check access, sample materials and terrain at the scale of the equipment.</p></div><div class="card highlight-cyan"><h3 class="editable">Add mission-specific evidence</h3><p class="editable">Incorporate high-resolution terrain and lighting where available, and document the measurements needed before any engineering decision.</p></div></div></div></section>
